(Problem statement)#h2{text-align:center}

 Citizen (community) science has been emerging as a method for crowdsourcing research data, but reliably processing its results has mostly remained the privilege of dedicated public or private academic institutions, with insular standards of peer review maintained by authoritative scientific journals. Beyond being as such fragmented, these standards are also subject to reviewer bias, and have notable "Great Divides", between eg. "the" sciences and humanities ((Snow 1959)@/Snow_1959_Two_cultures.pdf), "natural" and "social" sciences (Richter et al. 2022), and many further internal ones upon closer inspection. As a result, even common currencies of "the scientific method" such as replicability, falsifiability or generalizability, forming the basis for our trust in science, are seeing inflation in certain disciplines, whether by intention or through exposure in the recent "replication crisis". Without centralized regulation, the natural solution would be more interdisciplinary collaboration, or effective transdisciplinary mediation. 

 Techno-Anthropology, positioned right along the greatest of these divides, took on a great responsibility to reconcile some of these insular cultures ("build bridges"; Børsen_2013@Børsen_2013_Techno-anthropology_interdisciplinarity.pdf), yet over its decade of existence quickly surrendered this challenge in favor of a conservative disciplinary status, aligning with narrow anthropological standards of hermeneutic analysis in "localized" ethnographies, best suited to create further divides rather than closing any. Granted, the mistake here isn't necessarily obvious: anthropology has long touted itself as "the most humanistic of the sciences, and the most scientific of the humanities" ((Wolf 1964)#fragment/link("/epistemology/Wolf_1964_Anthropology.jpg","Wolf 1964")), inspiring faith in its harmonizing predicament. But faith is rarely a reliable epistemology, and certainly not one commonly qualified as such by any scientific standard. And indeed, by the millenium anthropology itself was found deeply divided among evolutionary and social epistemologies, almost unbeknownst to their practicioners unless confronted directly ((Fearn 2008)@/cybernetics/Fearn_2008_the_great_divide.pdf). 

 With an overabundance of hermeneutic theories, volatile terminology of "predatory abstractions" ((Debaise & Keating 2021)@/Debaise_Keating_2021_SpeculativeEmpiricismNatureandtheQuestionofPredatoryAbstractions.pdf) such as "infrastructuring", "institutioning" and "value inscription", we find our field at odds with technological realities. Under naive pretenses of "facilitation", and even "intervention", we impose limitations and contradictions in the way of organic progress, "demonizing" allies from technical fields and assuming a "heroic" stance in our appropriation of the "designer" role ((Hyysalo et al. 2016)@/Hyysalo_2016_The_New_Production_of_Users.pdf). By antagonizing traditional scientific narratives for taking a "view from nowhere, while claiming to see comprehensively" (ignoring widespread introspections about epistemic humility in eg. classical phenomenology), Donna Haraway ((1988)@/Haraway_1988_Situated_knowledges.pdf) crafted a double-edged sword for social sciences: standpoint epistemologies are only as good as naive realism unless actually accounted for. 

 In this spirit, I am setting out to test a prototype where researchers of any ilk (citizen or professional) can self-disclose their assumptions, methods and paradigms while recording and analysing data, in order to provide transparency in peer review (or peer production). This is a continuation of my internship work, where I created a platform for critical web development ("JSInterface"; https://jsinterface.org/author/blik/@https://jsinterface.org/author/blik/) modelling the same principles along with autonomy, accountability and standards-compliance to challenge contemporary industrial norms of "configurational" technology and participatory design (or restoring its original intent in Scandinavian regulations of the 70's, defending worker rights against "de-skilling" amid rapid diffusion of innovation - as opposed to astroturfing democratic justifications for its acceleration). The internship provided me with collaboration from two makerspaces in Germany (IndieWeb.org) and the US (Uncoded.org), who will help me through the design and testing phases of the prototype (with more networking also planned). 

 The research question I will therefore seek to answer: 

 How can a web-based data-visualization platform help establish and sustain scientific standards in citizen science communities? 

(Problem analysis)#h2{text-align:center}

(Bifurcation of nature)#h3

 Techno-anthropology denotes scientific investigations of how technology extends human agency. It is common to be enthralled by how latest digital technologies in particular, or the industrial revolution in general seem to disrupt our evolution, but technology has been a continuous defining feature for humanity since printing, agriculture or stonecraft hundreds, thousands and millions of years ago ((Mumford_1964)@/epistemology/Mumford_1964_Myth_of_the_machine.pdf{"style":{"#Mumford_1964+.pdfjs .canvasWrapper>svg>g>g:last-of-type":{"opacity":0}}}). Being instrumental to traditional disciplinary activities (mathematics, physics, chemistry, biology), these material "revolutions" have been recognized by "modern" sciences in separation from the Cartesian mind-body dualism, but as we'll see in traces of the recent "speculative (re-)turn" of realism, the body itself has been indistinguishable from a technology for language ((Wittgenstein 1953)@/Wittgenstein_1953_Philosophical_Investigations.pdf) and intellect ("intuition"; (Whitehead 1929)@/Whitehead_1929_Function_of_Reason.pdf; figure 1). The latter recognition is the most peculiar one, as it first proposed the agency of mind over body ("transcendentalism") in its "process ontology". In Europe, Husserl called his similar, parallel innovation "transcendental phenomenology", both in an effort to solve an epistemological crisis they saw in dualism's "bifurcation of nature" (or in Husserl's words, "psychologism") holding our agency hostage to material determinism through our volatile grasp on it (idealism, subjectivism, hermeticism; cf. "normal science"). 
 Whitehead justified his transcendental proposition with a claim to parsimony, employing less induction to explain reality ((Whitehead 1928)@/Whitehead_1928_Process_and_Reality.pdf) than eg. Spinoza's divide monism. As opposed to the falsifiability of empirical sciences, he argued philosophies can only be validated by their overall "applicability", and his effective prediction of impending quantum physical theories presented a strong such case. 

