 Before we further unpack transcendental realism, we have to understand how by the 20th century, academia became strongly divided, with scientific rationalism facing constant criticism for "disenchanting" our lives (Vico, Rousseau, Neitzsche). While some recognized that "science" need not be antagonized, and multiple epistemologies (art or superstition) can coexist while it evolves through criticism (Comte, Weber, or Husserl and Whitehead themselves), growing civil unrest lead to increasing polarization, erupting the "science wars" between rationalism (Popper) and relativism (Kuhn) in 1962. 
 While Whitehead made an original proposition, Husserl borrowed his method of phenomenological reduction from the Pyrrhonists' concept of "epoché" ("suspension of judgement"), according to whom its mastery was supposed to yield "ataraxia", a peace of mind characteristic of a virtuous observer. The stoics (eg. Seneca) combined this with "apatheia", an absence of excessive judgement, together defining the "good deamons" implicit in the term "eudaimonia", a mode of "happiness" obtained through a virtuous life. His failure to comprehensively criticise this model in his eager disavowal of pyrrhonic skepticism (suggesting, fairly, that it was not the full picture), made his 'transcendental' turn ironically mistakable for a road back to idealism, leading to his disappointment in his students and peers rapidly falling for political pathos (idealism) in the turbulent times of the Weimar Republic. Heidegger went on to establish the highly influential existentialist or "hermeneutic" school, much to Husserl's disdain ("[Heidegger] may be involved in the formation of a philosophical system of the kind which I have always considered it my life’s work to render impossible forever.", (Kisiel 2007)@/epistemology/Kisiel_2007_Becoming_Heidegger.pdf{"style":{"#Kisiel_2007+.pdfjs .canvasWrapper>svg>g>g>path":{"opacity":0}}}). Roman Ingarden remained most faithful to Husserl's intentions, and his thesis about 'Intuition and Intellect in Bergson' in 1918 touched on the core issue, but unconvinced, he later stuck to a conservative realist tradition ((Ingarden_1945)@/epistemology/Ingarden_1947_Controversy_over_Existence.pdf, 1947@/epistemology/Ingarden_1945_Controversy_over_Existence.pdf). 
 Bergson's heritage became more fruitful in the analytic philosophical tradition where, "transcendentalism" already having a foothold (Emerson, William James), Whitehead could overcome the pathos of idealism. While phenomenology was meant as a method to describe reality and left subjectivity implied, Whitehead explicated the latter's constitution through these processes. To my knowledge, this "Husserl-Whitehead correspondence" has yet to be established.
