 Whitehead (1928) identified the linguistic "subject-object-predicate" model as a core misrepresentation in the contintental tradition. In his procedural model, immediate actualities perpetuate each other in "objective immortality" of becoming. He describes philosophy as the process of rational generalization (logical and coherent) from immediate experience (applicable and adequate), through imaginative faculty (as opposed to Bacon's inductive positivism). The two conditions of logical integrity and coherence are gratuitous enough that "a system of philosophy is never refuted; only abandoned", as criticism makes them inadequate, resulting in "bold denial of facts". In Cartesian dualism, the material division of the body-mind substance is inductive, as well as Spinoza's (and absolute idealists') monism of 'modes' of a 'causa sui'. Whitehead's solution is the abandonment of static 'substance-quality' descriptions in favor of dynamic processes ('concrescence'), restoring 'modalities' as their constitutive empirical occasions, and replacing the ultimate fact of 'God' with the process of 'creativity'. He categorizes philosophical overstatements as 'fallacy of misplaced concreteness' and the digmatism of 'ex absurdo' philosophical premises, unfalsifiable short of well-defined metaphysical categories (misplaced certainty, mathematical deductivism in Plato, Aristotle, Aquinas, D, S, Leibniz, Locke, Berkeley, Hume, Kant, Hegel; cf. Husserl). 
