 Given Whitehead's theory and Husserl's methodology, we can proceed to investigate epistemologies, as opposed to social orders. As alluded to earlier, the realist-idealist debate that Husserl failed to dissolve, existed already in Hellenism, where Stoicism and Skepticism, and earlier the Socratic tradition and Sophists were divided by the same controversy. Similar to idealists, the sophists and skeptics believed that a quest for objectivity is a fool's errand, and truth is at best relative, if not entirely subjective. In their times, divination (astrology, augury, oneiromancy, haruspicy &c.) was still an esteemed "scientific" method for predicting the future. In De Divinatione (44 BC), Cicero offered an implicit account of pseudo-science as theories claiming this title without suitable "demarcation" criteria, that coincide with ones supported, or at least undisputed by modern philosophers of science, suggesting a core heuristic set: internal logical consistency, coherent causality, integrity of principles, supportive empirical evidence (past or predicted), and engagement with criticism ((Fernandez-Beanato 2019)@/epistemology/Fernandez-Beanato_2019_Cicero_demarcation.pdf). Cicero's consistent modus tollens (P→Q,¬Q⊢¬P) refutation of genethliac astrology was formalized by Popper (1934) for the fourth principle as "falsifiability" (along with caveats of representativity; for its demonstration on astrology, see Dean & Kelly 2003), to distinguish it from positivist "verificationism". 

