(Digital epistemology)#h3

 The utility of digital technologies for scientific work is inherent in their origin, with Turing's invention of the binary computer serving to solve all "effectively computable" problems. This theory of computability became the Church-Turing thesis ((Turing 1937,@/epistemology/Turing_1937_Lambda_computability.pdf) proving that general recursive functions (Gödel 1933), imperative programming (Turing machines), and functional (lambda) calculus are equivalent, although leaving the exact demarcation of "effective computability" uncertain. The strong version of this thesis (Deutsch 1985) nonetheless forms the basis of digital physics, assuming that the universe is equivalent to a Turing-machine, devoid of non-recursive functions (Oddifreddi 1989). If not the case, hypercomputation (an "oracle") would be required to simulate reality beyond Turing-completeness (eg. the Halting problem), with quantum computers or the mind being open candidates (Penrose 1990). 

 The proliferation of digital "data" and "algorithms" in scientific research has been met with much trepidation in Techno-Anthropology: in their treatise of their "issue", (Seaver (2019))@/Anthropology/Seaver_2019_Digital_STS_26_Knowing_Algorithms.pdf and (Douglas-Jones (et al. 2021))@/Anthropology/Douglas-Jones_2021_Anthropology_of_data.pdf painted grim pictures about their reductive (and other malicious) natures through "quantification", while leaping to their black box appearances in centralized platforms of social media, without accounting for them on smaller scales. In reality, data and algorithms are just abstractions of information and processes, and being digital doesn't make them any more (or less) reductive than pen and paper (natural language), or any other technology - if not only by outpacing clock speeds of human cognition and memory (though it would be lax to discount phenomenologies of intuition, creativity or agency so early even against an AGI). 
 This categorical error shares roots with the qualitative-quantitative debate (QQD), which is (very) slowly getting resolved in discourses about "mixed-methods" research. It is yet an old tenet of Hegelian dialectics, that quantities represent quantites, and vice versa, as commensurable analytical categories (Nguyen 2017). Due these concerns nonetheless, Techno-Anthropology has been assuming a "complementary" role providing "thick" context to data collection in other disciplines, missing but the infinite regress of this problem implied when "coding" or "digitalizing" its own "data". 
 Luckily though the problem is also not in fact this frightening. Storage and computation offers exciting new possibilities, and pursuing them responsibly is exactly where techno-anthropology could come to the rescue, if it dared to involve (or respect) expert perspectives in "human-centered" design. 

