Methodology#h2{text-align:center}

(Taking "accountability" seriously)#h3 

 The analysis I have provided gives theoretical grounding to engage with the community of citizen scientists. Traditional ethnography claims "generating theories" is an inductive excercise from observations, making preliminary "problem analysis" spurious, creating and unresolved double bind internal to the field of techno-anthropology. For this reason, ethnomethodology rejects "theory generation" in favor of free philosophical analysis of descriptions, supposedly obtained through phenomenological (as opposed to eidetic) reduction. In an extreme (including his own) interpretation of Garfinkel's program, ethnomethodology would disperse the social sciences as "hybrid" intradiscipline of natural sciences ((Hammersley_2018)@/epistemology/Hammersley_2018_ethnomethodological-criticism-of-ethnography.pdf). 

 Framing my study as a breaching experiment puts it on the hot seat in terms of scientific methodology. Studying the epistemology of citizen scientists is itself an epistemological process, yet if I was doing a conventional ethnography, I would be breaching tradition if I began to elaborate on my accountability. My research question is ethnomethodological in essence: how can a data-driven analysis platform promote scientific standards? 

 Ethnographers have been called on to reflect on their accountability for decades both in its original form, "trespassing" exotic cultures (D'Andrade 2000), and in its modern form, assuming "critical proximity" in local communities (Steen 2015). Anthropology is inevitably a speculative exercise, not empirical. As such, it has to apply philosophy. The speculative turn provides an opportunity to take professor Gluckman (Andersen & Lee 1982) and technomethodology (Crabtree 2004) seriously. Anthropology is Not ethnography (Ingold 2007). 

 Conversation analysis adopted rigorous standards for context awareness (sequential interpretation, reflexive hermeneutic cycle - Heritage 1984, Watson 1997). 
 
 1. Ethnography applies social concerns that are at odds with natural attitudes of practice. Ethnomethodology restricts itself to understanding these very natural attitudes, that practicioners may take for granted. 
 2. Ethnographers take phenomena for granted, which ethnomethodology provides a description for (Cicourel 1964), or disputes their relevance upending social sciences altogether. Research questions make accounts partial, but ethnomethodology observes intersubjective processes, not claims. This unique 'study policy' has not yet been justified. 
 3. Ethnographers insisted on the scientific character of their work, despite applying arbitrary theories and common-sense categories. A corrective system based on falsifiability is the only insurance of concept validity. EM champions husserlian literal description, but it's not devoid of such representation issues either, and even lacks falsifiability. "Context" can refer to the research purpose and setting, driving observations and interpretation, or the situation of a phenomenon, mutually reflexive in a hermeneutic cycle. For ethnography, they are apodictic evidence. Clarification of social causality would weaken ethnomethodology's criticism. 
 4. Immediate experience is an illusion, but transparency is not possible or necessary. 
 
 Stoddart (1996) already argued that ethnographers ignore, or systematically hide their own interference in an environment, believing in the revalatory nature of first-hand experience. 
 "not only is the perspective adopted often at odds with participant understandings, but the accounts produced by ethnographers frequently explain away those understandings, implicitly or explicitly" 
 "conventional ethnography typically formulates what is going on in the settings studied in the terms of anthropological or sociological theory, rather than in those deployed by participants." 
 "not only is the perspective adopted often at odds with
participant understandings, but the accounts produced by ethnographers frequently 
explain away those understandings, implicitly or explicitly." 
 "The researcher’s version, constructed as it is from an unanalysed borrowing of descriptive apparatus from members of the field, potentially stands in opposition to some members’ versions of 'how it is', and yet can offer no good reason for its claimed superiority. [...] Contrary to their claim to have developed scientific concepts, ethnographer's categorizations are no different in character from those of members." (Andersen & Lee 1982). 
 "ethnographic accounts underplay the features of ‘depth, stability and
necessity that people recognise as actually inherent in the circumstances of their existence." 
 Cultures are rendered ‘constructions that partake more of the character of theoretical formulation than of realistic description’ (Bittner 1973). More specifically, Garfinkel et al. (1988) noticed sociological concerns taking the place of faithful accounts of activity. Since social scientists are primarily concerned with studying a field, not participating in it, their descriptions are necessarily arbitrary, or derived from a sociological agenda not constrained by practical engagement. Becker's (1963) study of jazz musicians promptly ignored everything essential about jazz music itself (Faulkner & Becker 2009, Tolmie et al. 2013). The Sokal hoax (1992), and the Grievance Studies Affair (2018) were no political disputes, but "indigenous" protests against such superficial representations of scientific work, and a methodological critique as such, as long as these fields make claims for a scientific title (Pollner and Emerson 2001). Ethnographer retorts nonetheless often defended their freedom to theorize (link@https://blogs.lse.ac.uk/gender/2018/10/29/study-your-grievances/, Cole 2023). 
 Button et al. (2015) argue that social sciences "feed off and at the same time, curiosly, seek to rival common-sense", creating an internal epistemic double bind, in "interpretive or scenic ethnography", whereas ethnomethodology is directly concerned with practical description, not explanation. Objects of inquiry remain artfully constituted by these actors. To make matters worse, ethnographers appeal to values, norms, prspectives, interests etc. as deterministic causes of actions, when situational actions are constituted through a much more complex filter. 

 In his article titled "taking technomethodology seriously", Crabtree ((2004)@/Crabtree_2004_Taking_ethnomethodology_seriously.pdf) advocates for ethnomethodology, a "theoretically-informed", spatially and temporally extended variation of ethnography rebased on Husserl's phenomenological program, positioning social science as a "hybrid" discipline applying philosophy to interpret activities as they unfold organically (as opposed to theorizing about their respectives subjects or objects). This offers a solution to old controversies in computer-supported cooperative work (CSCW), trying to reconcile general aspects of software in localized contexts. 
 As he also describes, prototype tests create perfect grounds for "breaching experiments", where such "ethnomethodologically-informed" ethnographies can be a powerful companion to technological innovation, documenting its affordances and constraints, and culture's complementary reactions of acceptance or reuse (Leonardi 2023). I will account for these observations in terms of Whitehead's process philosophy, detecting occasions and concrescence to describe potential or realized benefits and externalities. 

 Through the prototype, I get to directly observe and record these activities, generating a digital blueprint along with my participant observations to analyse. 

 Focus of observations: 
- Data provision (How do you interpret the data source?) 
- Algorithm intuition (What are their research questions?)
- Result interpretation (What do they glean? What do they miss?) 
- Reflections: Anything they didn't understand? 
- Feedback: What would they change? 

